From 2004da5e742863fc462205ff90f346b62d1ddd70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:27:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] daemon: follow running builds' logs and resume
 GetBuildLog at an offset

GetBuildLog answered only with the file Nix kept after a build, from its
first byte. A dashboard needs the log of a build while it runs, a way to
resume a call that broke, the end of a large log without its middle,
and a way to tell one build's log from a retry's.

The request gains offset, follow, assign_id, limit and tail, and the
answer an info message first and an ended flag last. A node answers
from the build's spill while it runs here (LIVE), else from Nix's kept
file (KEPT), else NOT_FOUND. LIVE renders the spill as Nix keeps the
log, so a line number taken while a build runs is the same line after
it ends. The daemon's own messages become notes, which BuildDerivation
clients still see but the build log leaves out. The spill keeps an
index entry per 64 KiB, so a reader starts near any offset.

A follow call waits on the build's condition variable and sends what
arrived about ten times a second, with an empty message every 30 s on a
quiet build. It ends with ended when the build finishes, is revoked or
given back, and with UNAUTHENTICATED when a bearer token expires.

With assign_id set, LIVE answers only for that assignment, and the node
skips KEPT once another build of the derivation started here, since Nix
truncated that file. The answer is then NOT_FOUND with the newer build
in x-nix-rebuilding. Every answer carries x-nix-node, so a caller
behind the balancer can tell who answered.

The node decodes a .bz2 kept log once into an unlinked temp file that
every call for it reads (log-cache.hh), at most 8 logs and 2 GiB. A
node serves 32 calls at once, 8 per caller, and decodes 2 logs at once.
GetBuildLog moves to build-log-service, and the log store's uploader
reads kept logs through its readKeptLog.

The call needs only the watch role, because build logs are for anyone
who can watch the farm. New metrics count calls by source and code,
bytes, and open follow calls. nix_grpc_rpcs_total counts a caller
that an access rule lets only watch under the rule's pattern, such as
cn="dashboard-*", so viewers that a proxy names one by one add no series
each. Every other caller keeps its own name.
